<BaseLayout
  title="About AttoLang"
  description="The motivations behind AttoLang and the Oresoftware Dev House building it."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="kicker">About</p>
    <h1>Isolation is a language feature.</h1>
    <p>AttoLang is being built around the idea that concurrency, code loading, ownership, and trust boundaries should reinforce one another instead of being separate subsystems.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="prose">
      <h3>Motivations</h3>
      <p>
        Modern runtimes routinely need to host many independent jobs inside one machine: app plugins,
        serverless functions, actors, background tasks, user-authored code, and hot-reloaded modules.
        Process isolation is strong but can be expensive; shared memory is cheap but easy to misuse.
        AttoLang is exploring a middle ground where the compiler and VM cooperate on isolation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal is not merely memory safety. The language model should make it difficult to create
        accidental ambient authority, dangling references, unsafe actor messages, or mutable data
        races—and it should make the safe path ergonomic enough to use by default.
      </p>

      <h3>About the name</h3>
      <p>
        AttoLang was formerly known as Oreslang. Some GitHub repository names, source-file
        extensions and compatibility identifiers still use the original name while the
        project migrates. These historical names do not change the current AttoLang identity.
      </p>

      <h3>Oresoftware Dev House</h3>
      <p>
        AttoLang is authored by <a href="https://github.com/oresoftware">Oresoftware Dev House</a>,
        with the compiler and related projects collected under the
        <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang">ores-truffle-oreslang GitHub organization</a>.
      </p>
      <p><a href="/#about">← Back to the About section on the home page</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
